### Why do prefix lookups need a special structure?

Doing `LIKE 'ho%'` can use a B-tree index but still returns and ranks many rows per keystroke. A trie or edge-ngram index turns prefix search into a direct traversal.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete;

import java.util.*;

public class TrieNode {
    Map<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    List<String> topSuggestions = new ArrayList<>(); // pre-ranked top K
}

public class PrefixTrie {

    private final TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

    public void insert(String phrase, int score) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : phrase.toLowerCase().toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.computeIfAbsent(c, k -> new TrieNode());
            // Update top suggestions at each prefix node
            updateTopK(node, phrase, score);
        }
    }

    public List<String> suggest(String prefix) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        for (char c : prefix.toLowerCase().toCharArray()) {
            node = node.children.get(c);
            if (node == null) return List.of(); // no match
        }
        return node.topSuggestions; // pre-ranked, no sorting needed
    }

    private void updateTopK(TrieNode node, String phrase, int score) {
        node.topSuggestions.remove(phrase);
        node.topSuggestions.add(phrase);
        // Sort by score and keep top 5
        node.topSuggestions.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(this::getScore).reversed());
        if (node.topSuggestions.size() > 5) {
            node.topSuggestions = new ArrayList<>(node.topSuggestions.subList(0, 5));
        }
    }

    private int getScore(String phrase) { return phrase.length(); } // simplified

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PrefixTrie trie = new PrefixTrie();
        trie.insert("home loan", 950);
        trie.insert("home insurance", 720);
        trie.insert("housing scheme", 680);
        trie.insert("car loan", 850);

        System.out.println("Prefix 'ho': " + trie.suggest("ho"));
        System.out.println("Prefix 'hom': " + trie.suggest("hom"));
        System.out.println("Prefix 'ca': " + trie.suggest("ca"));
    }
}
```

```text
Prefix 'ho': [home loan, home insurance, housing scheme]
Prefix 'hom': [home loan, home insurance]
Prefix 'ca': [car loan]
```

### How do you debounce client requests?

Wait a short idle period after the last keystroke before sending. Cancel in-flight requests when new keys arrive.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete.client;

// JavaScript-style debounce (conceptual Java equivalent)
public class DebounceExample {
    // Typing "home loan" without debounce: 9 requests (h, ho, hom, ...)
    // With 200ms debounce: 1-2 requests ("home" after pause, "home loan" after pause)
    // Savings: 80%+ fewer requests

    // Cancel stale in-flight responses:
    // If user types "ho" then immediately "hom",
    // discard the response for "ho" so it does not overwrite "hom" results.
}
```

### How do you cache hot prefixes?

A small number of prefixes account for most traffic. Cache them in Redis.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete.cache;

import java.util.List;

public class PrefixCache {

    private final RedisClient redis;
    private final PrefixTrie trie;

    public PrefixCache(RedisClient redis, PrefixTrie trie) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.trie = trie;
    }

    public List<String> suggest(String prefix) {
        // Check cache first
        List<String> cached = redis.getList("autocomplete:" + prefix);
        if (cached != null) return cached;

        // Cache miss: query trie, populate cache
        List<String> suggestions = trie.suggest(prefix);
        redis.setList("autocomplete:" + prefix, suggestions, 300); // TTL 5 min
        return suggestions;
    }
}
```

### How do you rank suggestions?

Each phrase has a score combining popularity (search frequency) and recency (trending phrases surface). The score is precomputed at each trie node.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete.ranking;

public class SuggestionScorer {

    public int computeScore(String phrase, long searchCount, long daysOld) {
        // Popularity: higher search count = higher score
        int popularity = (int) Math.min(searchCount, 1000);
        // Recency: newer = higher boost
        int recencyBoost = daysOld < 7 ? 200 : daysOld < 30 ? 100 : 0;
        return popularity + recencyBoost;
    }
}
```

### How do you handle scale and updates?

Build the trie offline from query logs and product catalogs. Usage events stream in and refresh scores periodically.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete.build;

public class TrieBuilder {

    public PrefixTrie buildFromQueryLogs() {
        PrefixTrie trie = new PrefixTrie();
        // Read query logs: "home loan" searched 950 times
        trie.insert("home loan", 950);
        trie.insert("home insurance", 720);
        trie.insert("fixed deposit", 880);
        // Load into memory on each suggestion server
        return trie;
    }

    // Background: usage events update scores without blocking reads
}
```

### What about fuzzy prefix matching?

Layer fuzzy matching on carefully, only when exact prefix returns too few results.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete.fuzzy;

public class FuzzyPrefixSearch {

    public List<String> suggestWithFuzzy(String prefix) {
        List<String> exact = trie.suggest(prefix);
        if (exact.size() >= 3) return exact; // enough results

        // Too few exact matches: try fuzzy (edit distance 1)
        // "hoe" -> try "hom", "hob", "hoe" variants
        // More expensive, use sparingly
        return fuzzySearch(prefix, 1);
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you personalize autocomplete per user? How do you handle offensive or blacklisted suggestions? How do you shard the trie for a very large vocabulary? What is the difference between a trie and an edge-ngram index in Elasticsearch? How do you A/B test different ranking algorithms?

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer personalizes autocomplete by blending a per-user model (recent searches) with the global popularity model, shards the trie by first character for a large vocabulary, and A/B tests ranking algorithms by measuring click-through rate on the second-ranked suggestion versus the first.

```java
package com.bank.autocomplete;

public class SeniorAutocompleteGuidelines {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior autocomplete guidelines:");
        System.out.println("  1. Personalize: blend user-recent + global-popularity scores");
        System.out.println("  2. Shard trie by first character across servers for scale");
        System.out.println("  3. Blacklist: filter offensive or confidential suggestions");
        System.out.println("  4. A/B test ranking: measure click-through on top-K results");
        System.out.println("  5. Edge-ngram in Elasticsearch: alternative to in-memory trie");
        System.out.println("  6. Latency budget: trie lookup < 1ms, total with cache < 5ms");
        System.out.println("  7. Monitor: cache hit rate, p99 latency, suggestion click rate");
    }
}
```

